WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:01.735
We've been following breaking news

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:02.602
out of Tanzania

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.169
this morning

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.904
where several people have been rescued

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:06.740
after a commercial plane

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.375
with a number of passengers on board

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:10.243
crashed into Lake Victoria.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:11.911
Yes, really remarkable pictures

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:12.479
that we're seeing.

00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:13.680
You can see passengers

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.082
standing on the wings of the plane

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.385
as rescue boats ferry them back to shore.

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:19.486
A spokesperson

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:20.520
for Precision Air,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.187
which owns

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.889
the aircraft, says a plane went down

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:23.923
near an airport

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:26.493
located on Lake Victoria's western shore.

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:28.261
And in a tweet, Tanzania's

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.929
president sent his condolences

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:30.663
to those affected

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:31.965
and urged calm

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:33.600
while rescuers continue

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.901
to search for survivors.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:35.802
Let's talk more about this

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:37.270
now with Mary Schiavo.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.304
She is a former inspector

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:39.539
general for the U.S.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.074
Department of Transportation

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:43.443
and a CNN transportation analyst.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:44.077
Hi there, Mary.

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.745
So just looking at the pictures,

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:47.147
what do you assess, at least in terms

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.015
of how the pilot reacted

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:50.150
and what may have happened?

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:52.485
Well, yes,

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:53.253
they were taking

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.555
off and taking off all over water.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:56.489
I mean, there are.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:57.490
That's the most dangerous

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.725
part of the flight, of course.

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:00.827
And this plane an ATR,

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.529
they used to fly in the United States,

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:04.297
but they really don't anymore.

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:06.099
There were a couple of crashes

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:08.234
and especially crashes and icing that

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.371
really saw them pass out of service

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.705
from the U.S..

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:14.808
But the remarkable thing is,

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:15.809
as as you reported,

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.143
that people were standing on the wings

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.745
because planes

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.313
you know, don't make very good boats.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:20.880
And often

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.282
they think everyone remembers

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.349
the miracle on the Hudson

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:24.617
where the plane people

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.618
were standing on the wings

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:26.719
on the miracle on the Hudson.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.354
But what people didn't realize is

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.390
a lot of boats came quickly

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.591
and they got lines on

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.259
the aircraft, pulled the lines

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.228
taut to keep it from sinking.

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:36.663
So the fact that they were able to get

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:38.965
the pilot was able to get the plane down

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.201
without crashing into the water,

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:41.968
but looks rather

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.937
like landing in the water

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:45.605
is is really pretty good

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:47.173
without knowing much more than that

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.007
at this point.

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:49.843
So the fact that 26

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:50.977
already have been rescued

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:53.313
out of 43 passengers on board,

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:56.883
that's very good for a crash or landing,

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:57.784
if you want to call it that, a

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:58.551
water landing

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.387
Yeah, we're just getting that information

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:02.822
now from the airline

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:05.758
and the Tanzanian authorities.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:08.261
It appears as though

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.729
they are still in communication.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:10.763
They were able to communicate

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.432
with the pilots in the cockpit

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:14.968
even when the plane was submerged.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.604
Can you walk us through the process

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:20.807
of this kind of a rescue or

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:22.108
an emergency landing

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.877
as the plane is going down?

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.178
Where are the flight attendants

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.046
supposed to be doing?

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.615
Well, flight attendants,

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.550
because water's involved

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.351
there, couple things.

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:31.851
First of all,

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:32.852
they have to get them ready

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:36.122
for a brace landing, for a crash landing,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.190
if you will.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.058
And then they also have to get people

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.693
into now on a plane of this

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:41.628
size,

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:42.362
depending on

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:44.731
whether it usually operated over water.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.766
It may not have life vests,

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:47.934
but they may have to use for

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:50.236
flotation is their seat cushion.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:52.172
But if they regularly fly over water,

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.373
then they would have had life.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.908
This would have been required

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.043
depending upon the rules of the nation.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:58.211
So the flight attendants

00:02:58.211 --> 00:02:58.978
would have had to been

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.046
getting them in their lives.

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.414
This if they had them,

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:02.448
but instructing them,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:03.416
do not do not

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.852
do not open them in the cabin.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:06.352
You can't

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.388
you have to wait until you get out

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:09.355
because otherwise you could

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:10.523
get trapped in the cabin.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.592
So they have double emergency duty

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:12.992
to do

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:13.893
when it's suspected

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.462
that they're going to land on water.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.198
But since you're standing on the wing

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:21.301
and that appears they were able

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:23.169
to get those messages across

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.437
and get them out.

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:26.372
Now, the problem also comes in,

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.575
some of the plane is submerged,

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:29.175
of course.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.644
And so getting people out once they're

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:33.213
underwater is very difficult.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.549
You know, lighting becomes an issue

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:37.784
that's a real problem.

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:40.186
And so they literally would have to help

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:40.653
and help

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:42.789
lead people, get people out of the plane,

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:43.456
which has happened

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:44.657
in other water landings.

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.392
The flight attendants

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:49.095
you know, rescue rescue lifeguards.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.332
Yeah, it's a total of 43 people on board.

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:53.733
And so far, 26

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.401
and more than half have been rescued

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.070
that is good news.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.104
Just want to ask you about this.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:58.905
I found this interesting.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.540
A local official

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.474
was quoted as saying

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:02.508
that they are trying to check

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.245
if the wheels of the plane hit the ground

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:06.813
under water.

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:07.981
And just judging from the pictures,

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:10.283
you can see the water is pretty shallow.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:11.551
People are standing up

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:13.653
to some people's knees and waist.

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:14.854
Is there a chance?

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:15.622
Does it look like to you

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:16.923
that the wheels did

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.825
hit the ground out of that lake?

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:19.559
Yes.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:20.727
That would explain why

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:21.361
it didn't sink,

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:24.230
because planes sink very, very quickly.

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:26.432
And that would explain also

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.868
why if the plane is resting on the ground

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:29.235
and that

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.870
people are still able to get out,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.105
why there's why

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.373
there is at this point,

00:04:33.373 --> 00:04:34.274
without knowing more

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:35.375
there's a very good chance

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:36.242
that perhaps everybody

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:37.377
could have survived

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:38.411
so miraculous

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.079
that the wheels will touch the ground.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.648
Otherwise the plane would sink

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.483
very quickly, extremely quickly.

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:44.217
In fact,

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:44.450
You know,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.985
and when I went to flight school,

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:46.986
the instruction was

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:49.455
if you're landed on water

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.023
and you're,

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:50.256
you know,

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:51.391
get your passengers

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:53.593
and you out, don't for any reason

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:55.461
if the plane even if it hasn't sunk

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.930
yet, do not go back to that aircraft

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.232
because it will sink quickly.

